First, compute the average kinetic energy:

\[
E = \frac{3}{5}E_F = \frac{3}{5}(7\ \text{eV}) = 4.2\ \text{eV}
\]

Convert to joules:

\[
4.2\ \text{eV} \times 1.602\times 10^{-19}\ \text{J/eV} \approx 6.73\times 10^{-19}\ \text{J}
\]

Using \(E = \frac12 m v^2\):

\[
v = \sqrt{\frac{2E}{m}}
= \sqrt{\frac{2(6.73\times 10^{-19})}{9.11\times 10^{-31}}}
\approx \sqrt{1.48\times 10^{12}}
\approx 1.21\times 10^6\ \text{m/s}
\]

This matches choice B.

A common pitfall is using the Fermi energy directly instead of \(\frac35 E_F\), or forgetting to convert eV to joules. Also, the average speed here is a root-mean-square-type estimate from average kinetic energy, not a precise thermal distribution average.

